i called it mannequin madness because at  first i thought i might be a little  crazy doing this  i'm a woman with no experience in an  industry that no one's really heard of  everyone kind of looked at me a little  side eye what are you doing for a living  [Music]  my name is judy i'm the owner of  mannequin madness in oakland california  we sell new mannequins recycled  mannequins and we put together diy  projects involving the use of a  mannequin  [Music]  mannequin madness began as an accident  in 2000 the time i was working at a dot  com startup and everybody there was a  serial entrepreneur now at that point no  one looked like me who was an  entrepreneur i had never thought about  entrepreneurship but i thought this  could be maybe an interesting little  side hustle  i realized that department stores were  throwing away mannequins if they were  closing or remodeling i knew there was a  need for a secondary market for these  used mannequins but the stores didn't  want to deal with that so i started with  50 mannequins that i bought from the  seller on craigslist and six month  period of time i went to 500 mannequins  in my inventory so we went from renting  to selling we started using our photo  studio where people could come in to  rent to take pictures and then when  e-commerce came along i was able to  apply new technology strategies to an  old school business and that really  propelled me to a whole new level of  success  mannequins are made of materials that do  not biodegrade fiberglass plastic  styrofoam metal these things do not need  to be put in the landfill but prior to  us that was kind of generally what most  stores would do so we make it easy we  make it free for them i have a network  across the country so i can pretty much  recycle mannequins in most major cities  throughout the us  i say we average about 300 000 pounds of  mannequins every three months  i had no idea it had this kind of  potential but i've always been big about  how can we repurpose something and  create some more value out of it so  that's really what drove me and making  money came along with that we're going  to be on target this year to reach a  million dollars in sales for a small  business like us  that's a big target  it's called mannequin madness for a  reason because you don't know what  you're going to see when you get here  we have a warehouse showroom our  inventory changes depending on whatever  store we're working with so today we got  a truckload of mannequins from a  department store next week we may get  something from a smaller boutique  people use mannequins for all kinds of  art stuff you know i have mannequins in  the garden i've had mannequins that i've  turned into lamps i even have a little  mannequin that's shaped like a little  butler that has a little tray i like  that there's a little space here where  we really like you said being creative  and allowing other people to be creative  as well  i feel this is the greatest time in  history for an african-american woman to  start a business there has never been so  much emphasis on support giving people  visibility even though there's a lot of  challenges in the 20 years i've been in  business this has been the best time  ever  you really need to have technology for  your business at my age i'm starting to  learn how to be on tick tock  look at my family i've never seen a  whole family of such interesting looking  mannequins  here i'm a mid-life woman i'm in my 60s  i'm far away from the fashion capital  all these things that technically i  should not be a success in doing this  and i am and that really makes me  excited  [Music]  you
 

